-- title:
--   Corollary 3.4: Central and semisimple-factor components stay in the cone
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   In an admissible structural presentation, let $W$ be a pointed generating invariant cone and $\mathfrak s=[\mathfrak l,\mathfrak l]$. Suppose $x=x_z+x_s\in W$ is ad-nilpotent, with $x_z\in\mathfrak z(\mathfrak g)$ and $x_s\in\mathfrak s$. Then
--
--   $$x_z\in W\quad\text{and}\quad x_s\in W.$$
--
--   The case $x_s=0$ is included. This permits the central and semisimple-factor contributions to be treated separately.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 8, Corollary 3.4: Central and semisimple-factor components stay in the cone

